{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791026068,"nonce":"Gi5NayIJXqzoagyeOwT49fohASY=","title":"A focused text field kept the window drawing 60 frames a second","message":"The caret blink was an AnimationScheduler ticker, which counts as animation, so while a field had focus the loop drew a frame every vsync. Measured with a window holding one focused field, 9 s: 547 frames and 61% CPU.\n\nThe blink, and the scroll thumb's stay before it fades, are now scheduler timers: due at a wall-clock time, not animation, and the window waits on the window until the next one is due. Switching exposed a second bug: flush reported only changes that need a relayout, so a purely visual write (the caret's opacity) never caused a frame; hover and colour changes had only worked because transitions are animations. flush now reports any write that changes what is drawn.\n\nAfter: one frame per blink (every 531 ms), 1.5% CPU.","files":null}]}